%N A401756 Least period of the outer billiards orbit of (2n-1,1) relative to the Penrose kite with vertices (-1,0), (0,-1), (sqrt(5)-2,0), (0,1).
%C A401756 Let K be the kite in the name. For a point p outside K that is not on a line through an edge of K, let v be the vertex of K such that K lies to the right of the directed line from p to v, i.e., det(p-v,w-v) > 0 for each of the other three vertices w, where det((x1,y1),(x2,y2)) = x1*y2-x2*y1. The outer billiards map is T(p) = 2*v-p, and a(n) is the least m >= 1 such that T^m(2n-1,1) = (2n-1,1). The opposite orientation gives the inverse map, so a(n) does not depend on the orientation.
%C A401756 Following Schwartz, K is called the Penrose kite: it is affinely equivalent to the kite of the Penrose kite-and-dart tiling. Schwartz (2007) proved that outer billiards relative to K has unbounded orbits; this answered the question, going back to B. H. Neumann and J. Moser, of whether outer billiards relative to some convex planar shape can have an unbounded orbit.
%C A401756 For every n the orbit of (2n-1,1) is periodic, so a(n) is well defined. Indeed, the orbit stays in (1+2*Z[sqrt(5)]) X (1+2*Z), a set that contains no point on a line through an edge of K. By Schwartz's results, such an orbit is either periodic or unbounded, and an unbounded one would meet [0,2] X {-1}, but only in points of a certain Cantor set (Comet Theorem). This Cantor set has no point with first coordinate in 1+2*Z[sqrt(5)], so the orbit is periodic; see the Proof link.
%C A401756 All terms are even. Indeed, T^m(p) = (-1)^m*p + 2*(v_m - v_(m-1) + ... +- v_1), where v_i is the vertex used in the i-th step. If T^m(p) = p with m odd, this gives p = v_1 - v_2 + v_3 - ... + v_m. For p = (2n-1,1), reducing first coordinates modulo 2*Z[sqrt(5)] and second coordinates modulo 2 shows that (-1,0) occurs an odd number of times, (sqrt(5)-2,0) an even number of times, and (0,-1) and (0,1) an odd number of times in total, so m is even, a contradiction.
%C A401756 It appears that a(n) == 2 (mod 4); this holds for n <= 41395036.
%C A401756 The reflection (x,y) -> (x,-y) maps K onto itself and conjugates T to its inverse, so it maps each orbit onto an orbit with the same period. Thus a(i) = a(j) whenever (2i-1,1) and (2j-1,1) lie on the same orbit or on mirror-image orbits; such repeated values form the horizontal dashes in the plot.
%C A401756 It appears that for every n either (6*sqrt(5)-2)*n - 8 < a(n) < (6*sqrt(5)-2)*n - 3 or a(n) > 311*n, and that the first case occurs for a set of n of density (5-sqrt(5))/4 = 0.6909830...; these terms form the lowest curve in the plot. For n <= 41395036 the first case occurs 28603275 times, a proportion of 0.6909832...
